First, A Set is one of the things available to us in Java's Collections framework.
So, A set is one way to group a bunch of like objects together and handle them as a single unit.
Eg: We can add items to a set, see if a set contains an item, or remove things from the set.
In java, it is used similar to ArrayLists. However, Sets do have some differences than Arraylists.

Eg: We want to create a variable that holds a collection of strings.
    Set <DATATYPE> names = new Set<DATATYPE(Optional)>;
    // ❌This is actually wrong as Set is an interface and not a normal class.
    // This is important as we can't create instances of interfaces.
    // We can only create instances of classes that implement those interfaces.

    // That is where Hashes come in: We use HashSet.
    Set <DATATYPE> names = new HashSet<DATATYPE(Optional)>;
These are the methods available to a Set:
- add()
- remove() -> pass in the item and not the index.
- size ()
- contains() -> returns true or false depending on whether the set contains whatever we pass it
- isEmpty() -> returns true or false
- clear() -> removes every element from the Set.
- forEach()
- iterator()
- addAll() -> a method to take any collection and add all of its items to another collection.
Note,
- A HashSet does not maintain the order that we add the elements to it. We also don't have a built in sort method
    for a HashSet.
- A set does not allow duplicates. Adding a duplicate element does nothing.

Final: A set is useful when we want to store a bunch of items and don't want duplicates while also not caring the
particular order our elements are in.

Another implementation of a Set in Java is:
- TreeSet -> Things are put in a tree in a correct order. Note that HashSet is a lot faster than TreeSet
- LinkedHashSet -> doesn't maintain a natural ordering but maintains insertion order.